The difference between training a model and fine-tuning a model lies in their scope and starting point:

1. Training a Model
Definition: Building a model from scratch using a large dataset.
Process:
Initialize the model weights randomly.
Provide extensive training data to learn features and representations.
Requires significant computational resources and time.
Use case: When no pre-existing model exists for your specific domain or task.
2. Fine-Tuning a Model
Definition: Adapting a pre-trained model to a specific task or domain.
Process:
Start with a model that has already learned general features (pre-trained).
Continue training on a smaller, task-specific dataset.
Usually faster and requires fewer resources.
Use case: When leveraging existing models like BERT, GPT, or ResNet for specialized tasks, such as sentiment analysis or medical image classification.
Overall Difference
Training builds a model from scratch.
Fine-tuning adjusts an existing pre-trained model to a new, specific task or dataset.